# Wisdom's own English for the Ashtavakra Gita, chapters 1 to 9. Edited from the Sanskrit.
EN = {
'1.1': "How does one come to know? How does freedom come about? And how is dispassion reached? Tell me this, my lord.",
'1.2': "If you want freedom, my child, leave the objects of the senses as you would leave poison. Take forgiveness, honesty, kindness, contentment and truth as you would take nectar.",
'1.3': "You are not earth, water, fire, air or space. To be free, know yourself as the witness of all these, made of awareness.",
'1.4': "If you set the body apart and rest in awareness, you will be happy, at peace and free of bondage this very moment.",
'1.5': "You are not a Brahmin or of any other varna, you belong to no stage of life, and you are nothing the eyes can see. Unattached and formless, you are the witness of everything. Be happy.",
'1.6': "Right and wrong, pleasure and pain belong to the mind. They are not yours, all-pervading one. You are not the doer and not the one who enjoys. You are free, always.",
'1.7': "You are the one seer of all, and you have always been free. Your only bondage is this: you see the seer as someone else.",
'1.8': "You have been bitten by the great black snake of the thought 'I am the doer'. Drink the nectar of trust in 'I am not the doer', and be happy.",
'1.9': "'I am the one pure awareness': let this certainty be a fire that burns down the forest of not knowing. Then be free of grief, and be happy.",
'1.10': "That in which this world appears, imagined like a snake on a rope, that joy beyond joy, that awareness: you are that. Be happy.",
'1.11': "One who thinks 'I am free' is free. One who thinks 'I am bound' stays bound. The old saying is true: as you hold yourself, so you become.",
'1.12': "The Self is the witness: all-pervading, whole, one, free, awareness itself, without action, unattached, without wanting, at peace. Only through error does it seem caught in worldly life.",
'1.13': "Meditate on the Self as unchanging awareness, one without a second. Let go of the mistaken idea that you are a reflection, and of every sense of outside and inside.",
'1.14': "My child, you have long been tied by the rope of 'I am the body'. Cut it with the sword of knowing 'I am awareness', and be happy.",
'1.15': "You are unattached and without action, shining by your own light, without stain. This is your bondage: you keep practising samadhi.",
'1.16': "In truth this world is pervaded by you and strung on you. Your nature is pure awareness. Do not fall into smallness of mind.",
'1.17': "Need nothing, change not, be full and cool at heart, deep in understanding and unshaken. Let your one leaning be toward awareness alone.",
'1.18': "Know that what has form is unreal and what is formless is unmoving. Taught this truth, you do not come back to birth.",
'1.19': "As a mirror is present inside the image it holds and all around it, so the Supreme Lord is present inside this body and all around it.",
'1.20': "As one all-pervading space is inside a pot and outside it, so Brahman, eternal and unbroken, is in every being.",
'2.1': "How strange. I am spotless, at peace, awareness itself, beyond nature. All this time I have been fooled by delusion.",
'2.2': "As I alone light up this body, so I light up the world. So the whole world is mine, or nothing is.",
'2.3': "Now that I have let go of the world along with the body, through some skill in the teaching I see the supreme Self.",
'2.4': "Waves, foam and bubbles are not different from water. In the same way the world, which comes out of the Self, is not different from the Self.",
'2.5': "Looked at closely, a cloth is only thread. Looked at closely in the same way, this world is only the Self.",
'2.6': "As sugar made from cane juice is filled with that juice, so the world, imagined in me, is filled with me through and through.",
'2.7': "When the Self is not known, the world appears. When the Self is known, it does not. When the rope is not known, a snake appears. When it is known, the snake is gone.",
'2.8': "Light is my own form. I am nothing other than that. When the world shines, it is I who shine.",
'2.9': "How strange. Through not knowing, the imagined world appears in me, like silver in a shell, a snake in a rope, water in the sun's glare.",
'2.10': "The world came out of me and will dissolve back into me, as a pot into clay, a wave into water, a bracelet into gold.",
'2.11': "How wonderful I am. I bow to myself. I do not perish. Even when the whole world, from Brahma down to a blade of grass, perishes, I remain.",
'2.12': "How wonderful I am. I bow to myself. Though I have a body, I am one. I go nowhere and come from nowhere. I stand pervading the world.",
'2.13': "How wonderful I am. I bow to myself. No one is as able as I am: without touching it with a body, I have held up this world for all time.",
'2.14': "How wonderful I am. I bow to myself. Nothing is mine, or everything that speech and mind can reach is mine.",
'2.15': "Knowledge, the known and the knower: these three are not real. I am the spotless one in whom they appear through not knowing.",
'2.16': "How strange. Duality is the root of sorrow, and there is no medicine for it except this: all that is seen is unreal, and I am one, pure awareness, without stain.",
'2.17': "I am awareness alone. Through not knowing, I imagined limits on myself. Reflecting on this always, I rest in what has no divisions.",
'2.18': "I have no bondage and no freedom. The error has ended, with nothing left to stand on. How strange: the world rests in me, and in truth it does not rest in me.",
'2.19': "This world, body and all, is nothing: that is settled. The Self is pure awareness alone. So where could imagining take hold now?",
'2.20': "Body, heaven and hell, bondage and freedom, and fear as well: all this is only imagining. What have I, who am awareness, to do with any of it?",
'2.21': "How strange. Even in a crowd I see no second. It has become like a forest to me. What is there to cling to?",
'2.22': "I am not the body, and the body is not mine. I am not the separate self. I am awareness. My bondage was this: the craving to keep living.",
'2.23': "How strange. In me, the endless ocean, the many waves of the worlds rise at once when the wind of the mind blows.",
'2.24': "In me, the endless ocean, when the wind of the mind falls still, the ship of the world sinks, by the bad luck of the trader on board, the separate self.",
'2.25': "How wonderful. In me, the endless ocean, the waves of living beings rise, strike each other, play and sink back, by their own nature.",
'3.1': "You have known the Self as one and indestructible, as it is. You know the Self and you are steady. How can you still take pleasure in piling up wealth?",
'3.2': "How strange. Love for objects, which are a mistake, comes from not knowing the Self, as greed for silver springs up when a shell is not known for a shell.",
'3.3': "You have known 'I am that in which the world rises like waves in the sea'. Why do you run about like someone poor?",
'3.4': "Even after hearing that the Self is pure awareness and lovely beyond words, a person gripped by sexual craving grows clouded.",
'3.5': "The sage knows the Self in every being and every being in the Self. It is strange that the sense of 'mine' still goes on in him.",
'3.6': "Settled in the highest non-duality and set on freedom, he still falls under desire and is shaken by old habits of pleasure. That is strange.",
'3.7': "He knows desire is the enemy of knowledge, he is weak, his last days are near, and still he longs for pleasure. That is strange.",
'3.8': "He has turned from this world and the next, he can tell the lasting from the passing, he wants freedom, and yet he is afraid of freedom itself. That is strange.",
'3.9': "Whether fed with pleasures or tormented, the steady one sees only the Self and is neither pleased nor angry.",
'3.10': "He watches his own body acting as if it were someone else's. Praised or blamed, how could such a large-hearted person be shaken?",
'3.11': "He sees this world as mere appearance and has lost all curiosity about it. How could the steady one be afraid, even when death is close?",
'3.12': "The great one's mind wants nothing, even when nothing is left to hope for. Content in knowing the Self, with whom can he be compared?",
'3.13': "Knowing by nature that what is seen is nothing, how would the steady one look at things as 'this to take' and 'this to throw away'?",
'3.14': "For one who has dropped inner impurities, who is free of opposites and of wishes, a pleasure that comes on its own brings neither pain nor delight.",
'4.1': "The steady one who knows the Self plays the game of experience. He has nothing in common with the confused, who carry the world like beasts of burden.",
'4.2': "The state that Indra and all the gods long for, poor in their longing: the yogi stands there and does not grow excited.",
'4.3': "Inwardly, one who knows That is not touched by merit or sin, as space is not touched by smoke, though smoke seems to fill it.",
'4.4': "The great one has known this whole world as the Self. He lives as things come. Who could stop him?",
'4.5': "Among the four kinds of beings, from Brahma down to a clump of grass, only the wise have the strength to give up both wanting and not wanting.",
'4.6': "Rare is the one who knows the Self as one without a second, the Lord of the world. He does what he knows, and has no fear from anywhere.",
'5.1': "You are tied to nothing. You are pure. What would you give up? Let this bundle of body and mind dissolve, and so go to rest.",
'5.2': "The world rises from you like a bubble from the sea. Know the Self as one, and so go to rest.",
'5.3': "Though seen, the world is unreal, and it does not exist in you, the spotless one: it is like the snake in the rope. Know this, and so go to rest.",
'5.4': "The same in pain and pleasure, full, the same in hope and disappointment, the same in life and death: being so, go to rest.",
'6.1': "I am endless like space. The world of nature is like a pot. Knowing this, there is nothing to give up, nothing to take and nothing to dissolve.",
'6.2': "I am like the great ocean. The world of many things is like a wave. Knowing this, there is nothing to give up, nothing to take and nothing to dissolve.",
'6.3': "I am like the shell. The world is imagined, like the silver. Knowing this, there is nothing to give up, nothing to take and nothing to dissolve.",
'6.4': "I am in every being, and every being is in me. Knowing this, there is nothing to give up, nothing to take and nothing to dissolve.",
'7.1': "In me, the endless ocean, the ship of the world drifts here and there, driven by its own wind. I am not troubled by it.",
'7.2': "In me, the endless ocean, the wave of the world rises or sinks by its own nature. I neither grow nor shrink.",
'7.3': "In me, the endless ocean, the world is only imagined. I am at complete peace and without form. In this alone I rest.",
'7.4': "The Self is not in objects, and no object is in the Self, which is endless and spotless. So I am unattached, without craving, at peace. In this alone I rest.",
'7.5': "How strange. I am awareness alone, and the world is like a magic show. So how, and where, could I imagine anything to reject or accept?",
'8.1': "It is bondage when the mind wants something or grieves over something, lets go of something or grabs something, is pleased by something or angry at something.",
'8.2': "It is freedom when the mind does not want and does not grieve, does not let go and does not grab, is not pleased and is not angry.",
'8.3': "It is bondage when the mind is attached to any one experience. It is freedom when the mind is attached to none.",
'8.4': "When there is no 'I', there is freedom. When there is 'I', there is bondage. Knowing this, lightly, take nothing and drop nothing.",
'9.1': "Things done and things left undone, the pairs of opposites: when have they ever ended, and for whom? Knowing this, grow weary of it all, let go, and keep no vows.",
'9.2': "My child, rare is the fortunate one whose wish to live, to enjoy and to know has been put out by watching how people live.",
'9.3': "All this passes. It is spoiled by the three kinds of suffering, empty, worth little, fit to be left. Certain of this, one becomes still.",
'9.4': "What time, what age of life is free of the pairs of opposites for anyone? Ignore them, live with what comes, and you reach the goal.",
'9.5': "Seeing how great sages, saints and yogis differ in their views, who would not grow weary of it all and fall quiet?",
'9.6': "One who comes to know the nature of awareness through weariness with the world, evenness of mind and reasoning, and so crosses the round of birth and death: is he not a guru?",
'9.7': "See the changes of the elements as only the elements, as they are. That very moment you will be free of bondage and stand in your own nature.",
'9.8': "Habit-tendencies are the world. So let all of them go. Dropping them is dropping the world. Then stay as you are, as things come.",
}
